WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.130
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:08.433
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.733 --> 00:00:16.865
Unit fourteen: Art and Society. In this unit, you will discuss the role and value of art in society.

4
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.408
censorship of art.

5
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:24.767
censorship of art means banning or controlling artworks considered offensive.

6
00:00:25.067 --> 00:00:29.063
Censorship of art has a long and controversial history.

7
00:00:29.763 --> 00:00:33.263
Your turn. Say: censorship of art.

8
00:00:35.963 --> 00:00:37.649
censorship of art.

9
00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:39.732
public art.

10
00:00:40.432 --> 00:00:44.139
public art means art placed in public spaces for everyone.

11
00:00:44.439 --> 00:00:47.239
Public art can transform a neglected street.

12
00:00:47.939 --> 00:00:51.079
Your turn. Say: public art.

13
00:00:53.779 --> 00:00:54.734
public art.

14
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.111
cultural value.

15
00:00:57.811 --> 00:01:02.481
cultural value means the importance of something to a society's culture.

16
00:01:02.781 --> 00:01:06.232
Traditional dance has enormous cultural value.

17
00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:09.770
Your turn. Say: cultural value.

18
00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:13.808
cultural value.

19
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:15.806
patronage.

20
00:01:16.506 --> 00:01:19.829
patronage means financial support given to artists.

21
00:01:20.129 --> 00:01:23.685
Royal patronage supported Khmer artists for centuries.

22
00:01:24.385 --> 00:01:27.014
Your turn. Say: patronage.

23
00:01:29.714 --> 00:01:30.564
patronage.

24
00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.841
commodification.

25
00:01:33.541 --> 00:01:38.072
commodification means turning something into a product to be bought and sold.

26
00:01:38.372 --> 00:01:41.289
Critics worry about the commodification of culture.

27
00:01:41.989 --> 00:01:45.465
Your turn. Say: commodification.

28
00:01:48.165 --> 00:01:49.387
commodification.

29
00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:51.419
street art.

30
00:01:52.119 --> 00:01:56.708
street art means art painted on public walls, often without permission.

31
00:01:57.008 --> 00:02:00.610
Street art in Phnom Penh tells stories about modern life.

32
00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:04.531
Your turn. Say: street art.

33
00:02:07.231 --> 00:02:08.441
street art.

34
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.974
cultural institution.

35
00:02:11.674 --> 00:02:17.516
cultural institution means an organisation such as a museum, theatre or library.

36
00:02:17.816 --> 00:02:21.127
The National Museum is a key cultural institution.

37
00:02:21.827 --> 00:02:25.142
Your turn. Say: cultural institution.

38
00:02:27.842 --> 00:02:29.388
cultural institution.

39
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:31.489
art market.

40
00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:35.164
art market means the buying and selling of artworks.

41
00:02:35.464 --> 00:02:38.543
The global art market is worth billions of dollars.

42
00:02:39.243 --> 00:02:41.582
Your turn. Say: art market.

43
00:02:44.282 --> 00:02:45.271
art market.

44
00:02:46.283 --> 00:02:48.434
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:53.125
Ms. Sarah, is street art really art, or just graffiti?

46
00:02:54.417 --> 00:03:00.401
That depends on whom you ask. Many artists whose work began on walls now exhibit in galleries.

47
00:03:01.717 --> 00:03:04.831
So the art market eventually accepts it?

48
00:03:06.117 --> 00:03:09.939
Often, yes, which raises questions about commodification.

49
00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.331
And what about censorship of art?

50
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:19.147
Every society draws lines, some of which are hard to justify.

51
00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:22.589
Who should decide what is acceptable?

52
00:03:23.883 --> 00:03:29.494
Ideally, cultural institutions in which artists and the public both have a voice.

53
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:34.246
Grammar focus: Advanced relative clauses.

54
00:03:34.546 --> 00:03:40.887
Advanced relative clauses include whom after prepositions and quantifiers with of which or of whom.

55
00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:46.893
It depends on whom you ask. Every society draws lines, some of which are hard to justify.

56
00:03:47.193 --> 00:03:48.635
Whose shows possession.

57
00:03:48.935 --> 00:03:55.080
Use 'whom' after prepositions and in formal writing; use 'of which / of whom' after quantifiers.

58
00:03:56.083 --> 00:03:58.840
Now read and listen. Who Is Art For?.

59
00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.835
Art has always reflected the societies in which it is created.

60
00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:10.410
For centuries, artists depended on patronage from kings, temples and wealthy families.

61
00:04:11.717 --> 00:04:17.803
Today, the global art market, much of which is controlled by a few collectors, sets high prices.

62
00:04:19.117 --> 00:04:24.100
Critics argue that this commodification turns culture into a luxury product.

63
00:04:25.383 --> 00:04:30.738
In contrast, public art and street art are free for anyone to enjoy.

64
00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:38.043
Many street artists, some of whom started illegally, are now invited to paint city walls.

65
00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:44.461
Censorship of art remains a sensitive issue in many countries.

66
00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:51.952
Ultimately, art is valuable not because of its price, but because of the conversations it starts.

67
00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:55.879
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:04:56.179 --> 00:04:58.852
Financial support given to artists is blank.

69
00:04:59.152 --> 00:05:04.175
A, patronage. B, censorship. C, commodification.

70
00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:10.752
The answer is A: patronage.

71
00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:16.096
The students, most of blank were tired, finished the test.

72
00:05:16.396 --> 00:05:20.246
A, who. B, whom. C, which.

73
00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.412
The answer is B: whom.

74
00:05:27.917 --> 00:05:30.961
There are many museums, some of blank are free.

75
00:05:31.261 --> 00:05:34.067
A, whom. B, which. C, that.

76
00:05:38.083 --> 00:05:40.350
The answer is B: which.

77
00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:44.639
Turning culture into a product to sell is blank.

78
00:05:44.939 --> 00:05:49.707
A, commodification. B, cultural value. C, public art.

79
00:05:53.717 --> 00:05:56.273
The answer is A: commodification.

80
00:05:57.783 --> 00:06:01.199
According to the text, why is art valuable?

81
00:06:01.499 --> 00:06:06.616
A, its price. B, the conversations it starts. C, its age.

82
00:06:10.617 --> 00:06:14.160
The answer is B: the conversations it starts.

83
00:06:15.650 --> 00:06:20.706
Now it's your turn. Part one: Should governments fund the arts with public money?

84
00:06:26.006 --> 00:06:32.127
Part two: Give your position, two reasons and one example from Cambodia.

85
00:06:38.427 --> 00:06:50.764
I believe they should. Art strengthens identity and creates jobs. For example, Cambodian classical dance, which almost disappeared, survived thanks to public and international support.

86
00:06:51.064 --> 00:06:53.784
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:06:54.783 --> 00:07:00.292
Great work today! You learned eight new words and advanced relative clauses.

88
00:07:00.592 --> 00:07:05.044
Today's value is creativity. Use your imagination when you play.

89
00:07:05.344 --> 00:07:08.054
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:08.354 --> 00:07:10.960
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

